WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Show the decisions, including the ones you rejected.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.485
The middle of the report explains the system.

3
00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:09.499
The temptation is to describe what you built in the order you built it.

4
00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:11.997
Readers learn more from why than from what.

5
00:00:12.277 --> 00:00:15.413
Two long-lived design-writing traditions agree on this.

6
00:00:15.693 --> 00:00:28.557
Ubl’s account of design docs at Google calls the alternatives-considered section one of the most important: it shows the trade-offs that led to the chosen design, which is what a reviewer is actually evaluating.

7
00:00:28.837 --> 00:00:46.992
Python’s PEP 1, which governs how Python language changes are proposed, asks for a Rationale section that explains why particular design decisions were made, and a Rejected Ideas section that records the alternatives and why they lost, so the same arguments don’t have to be repeated.

8
00:00:47.272 --> 00:00:53.203
For your capstone, a decision record can be three lines: the choice, the alternative, and the evidence.

9
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:57.429
“Hybrid search over dense-only: recall@10 rose from 0.

10
00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:59.160
71 to 0.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.643
83 on 180 labeled queries (paired difference 0.

12
00:01:03.923 --> 00:01:05.373
12, interval 0.

13
00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:07.125
07 to 0.

14
00:01:07.405 --> 00:01:09.347
17); cost unchanged.

15
00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:15.216
” A decision you made by judgment, without measurement, is fine too, as long as you say so.

16
00:01:15.496 --> 00:01:20.552
What reviewers distrust is a design presented as if it were the only possibility.
